  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cincinnati's pitching is in good form, but the Yankees lineup finally exposed itself on a day when depth mattered. This team doesn't lose games like this by accident. They lose them when early leads evaporate and the bats go silent in the innings where they needed noise most.",
    "what_this_means": "Will Warren gave them a legitimate start. Five and two-thirds innings, eight hits, two earned runs. That's a line you can win with. But Yarbrough surrendered four in the eighth, and by then the game had already turned. The pattern from inning three onward was unmistakable: runners reached base, nothing came of it. Twelve RISP opportunities. One converted. That's not execution. That's a locked door. They held for two innings, led 2-0, and watched Cincinnati score two in the third, four in the fifth, four more in the eighth. A one-run advantage became a nine-run hole. The margin disappeared fast because early damage doesn't recover in games like this.",
    "what_to_watch": "Chase Burns brings a 2.01 ERA into Cincinnati's lineup, and the Reds' .229 average gives Elmer Rodr\u00edguez a clear path tonight. One game does not reset inconsistency. But one sharp outing against a team that's struggling offensively can remind this lineup what precision looks like.",
